<?xml version="1.0" ?>
<!--
  detector_test.world — Minimal Gazebo Classic world for Stage-1/2 perception testing.
  Purpose: YOLO26 detector + LiDAR-camera localizer validation.
           No SLAM, no Nav2, no warehouse walls.

  ═══ Validated Semantic Target Set ═══════════════════════════════════════════
  One instance of each of the three enabled targets in
  config/semantic_targets.yaml. Nothing else is in the scene.

    semantic_name    detector_label    gazebo_model          Placement
    ─────────────    ──────────────    ────────────          ─────────
    person           person            person_standing       2.0 m @   0°
    trash_can        trash_can         first_2015_trash_can   2.2 m @ -45°
    chair            chair             VisitorChair           3.0 m @ +45°
  ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

  ─── Layout (top view, +X ahead of the robot, +Y left) ────────────────────

                            +Y (left)
                              │        [VisitorChair]
                              │          (2.12, 2.12)   3.0 m @ +45°
                              │        ╱
                              │      ╱
  Robot ──────────────────────┼────[person_standing]────→ +X
  (0, 0)                      │      ╲   (2.0, 0.0)      2.0 m @ 0°
                              │        ╲
                              │        [first_2015_trash_can]
                              │          (1.56, -1.56)  2.2 m @ -45°

  ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

  WHY THESE DISTANCES — each target sits inside its own measured band, and
  inside the localizer's LiDAR gate (localizer.yaml min_valid_range 1.0,
  max_valid_range 3.3):

    person     2.0 m  yolo26n p50 ~0.9 at 2.0 m side-on; the strongest of the
                      three. coordinator.yaml gates it at person_max_range_m
                      2.5, so it must sit below that.
    trash_can  2.2 m  F1 ablation: p50 0.45 at 1.5 m head-on, 0.42 at 2.0 m,
                      0.31–0.65 at 2.5 m. LiDAR beam_hit 1.00 at every pose
                      with a constant −0.22 m offset (the can's radius).
    chair      3.0 m  The chair FALLS BELOW the 0.35 bar when viewed head-on
                      at 1.5–2.0 m, so it is placed far. Its yaw matters more
                      than its distance and was measured here — see OBJECT 3.
                      coordinator.yaml gates it at 3.2 m.

  WHY ±45° AND NOT ALL IN ONE FRAME — the camera's horizontal FOV is 62.2°
  (localizer.yaml camera_hfov_deg), i.e. ±31.1°. Each target is therefore
  centred by rotating the robot to its bearing, and only one is in frame at a
  time. That is deliberate:

    * chair and trash_can are 90° apart, so they can NEVER appear in the same
      frame. yolo26n swaps those two labels when either one is clipped at the
      frame edge (the reason coordinator.yaml raises both promotion
      thresholds to 12), and this world removes that confound entirely.
    * the three objects are 1.62 m / 2.12 m / 3.72 m apart pairwise, all above
      semantic_map_memory's 1.5 m merge radius, so two targets can never be
      merged into one landmark.

  Rotate with teleop to bring each target to the centre of the frame:
      ros2 run turtlebot3_teleop teleop_keyboard
-->
<sdf version="1.6">
  <world name="detector_test">

    <!-- Directional sunlight — essential for texture-based YOLO detection -->
    <include>
      <uri>model://sun</uri>
    </include>

    <!-- Flat infinite ground plane -->
    <include>
      <uri>model://ground_plane</uri>
    </include>

    <!-- Physics: real-time stable -->
    <physics type="ode">
      <real_time_factor>1.0</real_time_factor>
      <max_step_size>0.001</max_step_size>
    </physics>

    <!-- Bright scene for camera-based detection -->
    <scene>
      <ambient>0.6 0.6 0.6 1</ambient>
      <background>0.8 0.85 0.9 1</background>
      <shadows>false</shadows>
    </scene>

    <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
         OBJECT 1 — Person
         semantic_name:    "person"
         detector_label:   "person"
         gazebo_model_name: person_standing

         Pose: (2.0, 0, 0) — dead ahead, 2.0 m from the robot
         Yaw: π — person faces the robot for maximum silhouette area
         ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
    <include>
      <uri>model://person_standing</uri>
      <name>test_person</name>
      <pose>2.0 0 0 0 0 3.14159</pose>
    </include>

    <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
         OBJECT 2 — Trash can
         semantic_name:    "trash_can"
         detector_label:   "trash_can"   ("traffic light" with the COCO weights, which
                                          have no trash-can class)
         gazebo_model_name: first_2015_trash_can

         Pose: (1.556, -1.556, 0) — 2.2 m at −45°, to the robot's right.
         Rotationally symmetric, so yaw is irrelevant.
         ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
    <include>
      <uri>model://first_2015_trash_can</uri>
      <name>test_trash_can</name>
      <pose>1.556 -1.556 0 0 0 0</pose>
    </include>

    <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
         OBJECT 3 — Chair
         semantic_name:    "chair"
         detector_label:   "chair"
         gazebo_model_name: VisitorChair

         Pose: (2.121, 2.121, 0) — 3.0 m at +45°, to the robot's left.
         Yaw +1.571 rad (+90°). Measured in this world, robot facing the
         chair, 200-frame samples per orientation:

             chair yaw   detected as "chair"   detected as "traffic light" (COCO weights, 2026-08)
             ─────────   ───────────────────   ───────────────────────────
              +1.571      227 @ conf 0.79       0            ← chosen
              −1.571      174 @ conf 0.63       0
              +3.142      179 @ conf 0.59       0
               0.000      182 @ conf 0.53       0
              +0.785      219 @ conf 0.48       0
              −0.785        0                 199 @ conf 0.64
              +2.356        0                   0
              −2.356        0                   0

         Two aspects are traps, which is why this yaw is pinned: at −0.785
         the chair is read as a TRAFFIC LIGHT on essentially every frame —
         the same label the trash can uses, which would make this world
         teach the exact confusion it is meant to avoid — and at ±2.356 it
         is not detected at all. +1.571 is both the most confident and the
         most stable.
         ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
    <include>
      <uri>model://VisitorChair</uri>
      <name>test_chair</name>
      <pose>2.121 2.121 0 0 0 1.571</pose>
    </include>

  </world>
</sdf>
